{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b93272701f4e2276","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'E001 question_specificity=0 is arguable: the rationale states a human\n10\t    must frame the utility-vs-log-volume trade-off before the question is decidable,\n11\t    mirroring the rubric -1 condition (criterion undefined, a human must frame it\n12\t    first); -1 may be the more rubric-accurate value. Does not change routing.'\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n15\t  description: E002 concentrates roughly three-quarters of the total estimated effort\n16\t    (audit + full manager-binary conversion + repo-wide CI lint rule + dev-cluster\n17\t    validation), approaching the two-week epic size ceiling; acceptable for a uniform\n18\t    mechanical change but the largest epic in the set.\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Completeness\n21\t  description: Backwards-compatibility/log-consumer regression validation is carried\n22\t    only by E002 (manager binary); E003 (cloudmanager, deployed on EKS/AKS/CoreWeave\n23\t    where CloudWatch ingests logs) has no explicit consumer-ingestion validation AC.\n24\t    The strategy NFR asks for a sample reconciliation error only, so this is a completeness\n25\t    nit.\n26\terror: null\n27\t---\n28\t# RHAISTRAT-8010 Decomposition Review\n29\t\n30\t## Review Summary\n31\t\n32\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n33\tRecommendation: accept\n34\t\n35\t## Criterion Details\n36\t\n37\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n38\t\n39\tTraceability matrix present in the decomposition summary and per-epic HLR Traceability sections exist in all four epics. All six P0 HLRs map: HLR1 (message-embedded identity → structured KV) splits correctly across E002 (manager) and E003 (cloudmanager); HLR2 (DSCI key migration), HLR3 (service controller resourceKind), HLR4 (15 action files across 4 tiers), HLR6 (module controller + 8 handlers) map to E002; HLR5 (cloudmanager controllers) maps to E003. P1 HLR8 maps to E004. P1 HLR7 (DSC/component controller resourceKind) is bundled into E002 with explicit justification — it touches the same log calls and implementation surface as the P0 conversion, so it is not a distinct deferrable feature; the split would duplicate the identical data structure. Priority inheritance is correct: E002/E003 are P0 (directly implement P0 HLRs); E001 is P1 (transitively enables only P1 HLR8, blocks no P0 work); E004 is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1). No priority collapse with deferrable features.\n40\t\n41\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n42\t\n43\tNo cycles. Diagram uses `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (E001/E002/E003 → E004), matching the stated convention. Frontmatter cross-check is exact: E001/E002/E003 have empty `dependencies`; E004 lists all three (E001, E002, E003); every diagram edge has a matching frontmatter entry and vice versa. Edge justification: E001 → E004 is a valid Rule 3 investigation gate with `gated_by: E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite`, and populated fallback — and `gated_by` target appears in E004's direct `dependencies`. E002 → E004 and E003 → E004 are Rule 11 docs-authoring ordering edges (valid, no priority inheritance). The absence of E001 → E002/E003 edges is correctly justified: the required field set for conversion is fixed by the HLRs, so the investigation does not gate the code work. E002 ∥ E003 is correct per Rule 15 (same repo, disjoint controller sets); the repo-wide CI lint rule overlap is consciously treated as rollout coordination, and E003's lint-based AC offers a static-analysis alternative, so no hidden artifact dependency exists. No konflux/license/repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2). No branch files exist, and none are needed: the E001 outcome changes E004's content (rewrite), not which epics exist.\n44\t\n45\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n46\t\n47\tSingle component (canonical `OpenShift AI Productization`, mapping from rhods-operator logged in the summary) and single implementing team (ODH Operator team); COO team correctly not given an epic (recipient of published convention only, per overlay 0008). Work-product type boundary respected: code implementation (E002, E003) separated from substantive content authoring (E004, docs-authoring); the release-notes item inside E002 is incidental documentation falling out of the code change. No scope duplication between E002 and E003 — disjoint controller sets (manager tiers vs. the three cloudmanager controllers), and the CI lint rule is single-sourced in E002 with coordination noted in E003. Minor: E002 concentrates roughly three-quarters of the strategy's estimated effort (audit + full manager-binary conversion + repo-wide CI lint rule + dev-cluster validation), approaching the size ceiling — acceptable for a uniform mechanical conversion where further splitting would create artificial boundaries, but it is the largest epic in the set.\n48\t\n49\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n50\t\n51\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: the field-set decision changes what E004 delivers (the guideline's documented field set), and E004 exists as downstream dependent with a true gate (`gated_by` + rewrite impact). E002/E003 are Implementations producing code/test/CI artifacts. E004 is an Implementation (docs-authoring) producing a published guideline. The only epic with non-null `gated_by` (E004) has both `gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and the gate genuinely changes the deliverable on failure (rewritten guideline content), so it is not a mere scheduling dependency. The only epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` (E004) has `gated_by` set to it. No misclassifications.\n52\t\n53\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n54\t\n55\tType-appropriate signal fields used throughout: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E002/E003/E004 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-contamination. Signal rationale files exist for all four epics and the rationales match frontmatter values. E001's `human_judgment_required: -2` is exactly right (the gating question is a PM/Observability governance decision), and `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the answer does not yet exist in any readable record. E002's `human_process_gates: -1` is justified by the dev-cluster log-consumer validation and SRE coordination. One arguable value: E001's `question_specificity: 0` — the rationale itself states \"a human must frame the trade-off weighting before it is decidable,\" which mirrors the rubric's -1 condition (\"criterion undefined (a human must frame it first)\"); the question is concrete and enumerable, so 0 is a defensible middle, but -1 is the more rubric-literal reading. This does not change routing (the epic is Low either way via `human_judgment_required`). Per the rubric, computed totals were not checked.\n56\t\n57\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n58\t\n59\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. All seven strategy ACs are carried: message-embedded conversion (E002 AC1, E003 AC2), DSCI camelCase migration with grep verification (E002 AC2), service controller resourceKind (E002 AC3), action files across all 15 files (E002 AC4), cloudmanager fields (E003 AC1), cluster-scoped namespace handling (E002 AC6), documented convention (E004 AC1). Rule-mandated ACs: Rule 24 technical-review-against-implementation AC is present on E004 (\"documented technical review sign-off\"). Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) does not apply — the NFR frames the change as additive log fields, not a capability replacement. Rule 25 is N/A (no konflux chain). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — cross-epic couplings are described in plain terms (\"the manager-binary conversion,\" \"the convention field-set investigation\").\n60\t\n61\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n62\t\n63\tAll strategy scope is covered: both binaries, all controller tiers (including the module controller tier restored by Staff Engineer input), the 15 action files, the CI lint rule, the DSCI migration with release notes and query examples, and external publication with COO tracking issues. Every open question is explicitly dispositioned (investigation, AC, planning note, or resolved-by-SME), and the strategy's risks are carried into epic scope (audit checklist + lint rule for missed paths; dev-cluster consumer regression + SRE alert coordination; early publication tension noted with a documented workaround). Out-of-scope boundaries respected (no COO adoption epic, no dashboards/alerting, no non-reconciliation paths). Docs-authoring: the strategy's Prerequisites list shows Documentation Support as \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\", but HLR8 explicitly requires the guideline, and E004 satisfies it either way. Minor: the backwards-compatibility/log-consumer regression validation is carried only by E002 (manager binary); E003's cloudmanager logs (deployed on EKS/AKS/CoreWeave where CloudWatch is a consumer) have no explicit consumer-ingestion validation AC — the strategy's NFR asks for only \"a sample reconciliation error,\" so this is a completeness nit, not a dropped requirement.\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb2518830a4fe5b1","agent_id":"a2f15ca84a888e9e0","session_id":"affdec6a-eca1-48e8-b648-ac73c57fc806","uuid":"fe2bd8d4-51c8-4b1f-bce4-0437e6ab2f5b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:39:21.752Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b93272701f4e2276","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ea391d34-4a54-42a4-b7da-0f1631645cb5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The review passes (13/14, accept). The issues list contains three minor issues. Per the revise-agent instructions: \"If the review passed all criteria with no issues to fix, skip to Step 3.\" Here the review passed but the issues list is non-empty. Hmm — the instruction says only fix issues the reviewer identified. The issues list has three minor items. Are these \"issues to fix\"?\n\nIssue 1: AI Implementability Scoring — E001 question_specificity=0 is arguable; -1 may be more rubric-accurate. The reviewer says it \"does not change routing.\" This is a judgment call — the reviewer flags it as arguable. The correction type \"Signal value incorrect\" — should I update the signal? The reviewer says 0 is defensible but -1 is more rubric-literal. Given the reviewer's own rationale mirrors the rubric's -1 condition, I think I should apply the fix: set question_specificity to -1 in E001's investigation_signals and update the rationale file. Note: do not update ai_implementability or score.\n\nIssue 2: Epic Boundaries — E002 is large but \"acceptable\". The reviewer says further splitting would create artificial boundaries. This is a note, not a required fix. I'll leave it as-is — reviewer explicitly deemed acceptable.\n\nIssue 3: Completeness — E003 lacks a consumer-ingestion validation AC; \"a completeness nit\". This one is actionable: add a consumer-ingestion validation AC to E003. That's a concrete fix — add an AC to E003 derived from the strategy NFR (sample reconciliation error / log-consumer ingestion validation on cloudmanager logs in CloudWatch).\n\nSo my plan:\n1. Read strategy file, decomposition summary, epic files E001, E002, E003, E004, and signal files.\n2. Fix issue 1: E001 question_specificity 0 → -1, update rationale in E001-ai-signals.md.\n3. Fix issue 3: add consumer-ingestion validation AC to