{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84478b8bb95224a2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# RHAISTRAT-8010 Decomposition — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n10\t\n11\t## Triage Outcome\n12\t\n13\tNot below-threshold: although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator) and a single implementing team (ODH Operator team), it spans distinct work streams — code implementation across two binaries (manager and cloudmanager) plus substantive documentation authoring (a new contribution guideline and publication of the convention for external operator adoption). Per Step 0, architecturally distinct work streams defeat below-threshold. Not docs-only: rhods-operator requires code changes.\n14\t\n15\t## Component Mapping\n16\t\n17\t- Strategy component **rhods-operator** (manager + cloudmanager binaries) has no entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Closest canonical parent-level component: **OpenShift AI Productization** (the RHOAI control-plane operator). All epics use this canonical name.\n18\t- Architecture context file exists (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`) → component is in the platform. No repo-onboarding / license-validation / konflux-onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 does not apply).\n19\t- The Cluster Observability Operator team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) appears in Impacted Teams as a recipient of the published convention, not as an implementer. Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies and adoption is voluntary — no epic is created for COO-team work. Publishing the convention and filing tracking issues in COO projects are scope of the docs epic. This is a deliberate non-application of the passive-component validation rule: COO adoption is discretionary external work, not validation of this strategy's deliverable.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Resolve the structured log field convention (fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind) | Investigation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs (structured identity, resourceKind, DSCI key migration) | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: convention field set\"] --> E004[\"E004 docs: convention guideline + publication\"]\n35\t    E002[\"E002 Implementation: manager binary logs\"] --> E004\n36\t    E003[\"E003 Implementation: cloudmanager binary logs\"] --> E004\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tCritical path length: 2 (e.g., E002 → E004).\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge (or non-edge) | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|----|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The strategy's open question \"should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category)?\" is owned by PM/Observability and its answer determines the field set the contribution guideline documents — it changes what the docs epic delivers. True gate, type `rewrite`: the guideline content follows the decided field set. Recorded via `gated_by` on E004. |\n46\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring ordering) | docs-authoring epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; the guideline describes what was built. Edge does not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n47\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring ordering) | Same as above. |\n48\t| No edge E001 → E002 / E003 | Rule 4 / Step 3 decision rule | The P0/P1 field set for the conversion work (name, namespace, resourceKind) is fixed by the HLRs; the investigation concerns optional additional fields only. Implementation proceeds identically regardless of the outcome, so the investigation does not gate the code epics. If additional fields are later adopted, extending already-converted call sites is incremental follow-up, not a rewrite. |\n49\t| No edge E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries in the same repo with disjoint controller sets → parallel. The CI lint rule (scope of the manager epic, applies repo-wide) creates a rollout coordination risk with the cloudmanager conversion — a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n50\t| No onboarding edges | Rule 20 (n/a) | rhods-operator exists in architecture context; no new component provisioning. |\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n55\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n56\t| HLR1 — message-embedded identity converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E002, E003 | Manager portion in E002, cloudmanager portion in E003. |\n57\t| HLR2 — DSCI migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase keys | P0 | E002 | DSCI controller is in the manager binary. |\n58\t| HLR3 — service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup are manager-binary controllers. |\n59\t| HLR4 — all action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E002 | All 15 action files are manager-binary paths. |\n60\t| HLR5 — cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E003 | AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine controllers. |\n61\t| HLR6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E002 | Module controller and its 3 action files are manager-binary paths. |\n62\t| HLR7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E002 (bundled) | Bundled with P0 work per Step 4: adding `resourceKind` to the same log calls touched by the P0 conversion is the same implementation surface — splitting would duplicate the identical data structure and touch the same files twice. |\n63\t| HLR8 — document convention as contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | E004 | Content gated by the E001 field-set decision. |\n64\t\n65\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n66\t\n67\t## Open Question Disposition\n68\t\n69\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n70\t|----|------|-----------|\n71\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind? (PM / Observability) | **Investigation epic E001**, gating E004 | Answer changes what the convention guideline documents (and any adopted fields become part of the convention the lint rule enforces). Does not change the P0 conversion work — required fields are fixed by HLRs. |\n72\t| Which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns? (SRE / Support) | Acceptance criterion on E002 | Strategy states this is identified during the log consumer regression validation step before merge; the work proceeds the same way regardless (identify and update alerts) — failure means fix-and-retry, not different epics. |\n73\t| Should cloudmanager be prioritized for the same release as manager, or follow subsequently? (PM / ODH Operator) | Planning note on E003 | Answer changes release scheduling only, not which epics exist or what they do. Cloudmanager is a separate epic precisely so it can be scheduled independently of the manager work. |\n74\t| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity? (ODH Operator) | Acceptance criterion (audit step) on E002 | The conversion work is identical regardless of the percentage — audit identifies call sites, all are converted. |\n75\t| Former question on component action file count (~16?) | Resolved by Staff Engineer input | All 8 components have exactly one action file; 15 total action files across 4 tiers confirmed. No investigation needed. |\n76\t\n77\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n78\t\n79\tStaff Engineer / SME Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. The strategy body already incorporates the corrections; logged for traceability:\n80\t\n81\t1. **Action file inventory corrected**: \"~16 component action files\" is wrong — exactly 8 component action files; 15 total action files across four tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module). Used throughout all epics.\n82\t2. **Module controller tier added**: the architecture description originally omitted the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and its 3 action files. Included in E002 scope (HLR6).\n83\t3. **Open question on action file count resolved**: 8/8 confirmed — question removed from the open question set.\n84\t4. **Confirmed as authoritative**: three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`); DSCI key names with file:line references; 5 service controllers including Setup; cloudmanager shared reconciler already provides name/namespace; camelCase field naming; external operator treatment per platform policy; cluster-scoped resource AC; effort estimate S.\n85\t\n86\t## Health Warnings\n87\t\n88\t- **Pending process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD, Performance Team) are \"Pending human review\". The decomposition assumes the strategy is approved as written; a materially different review outcome would require re-decomposition.\n89\t- **Docs ordering vs. \"publish early\" mitigation**: Rule 11 places the docs-authoring epic after all Implementations, but the strategy's external-adoption-delay risk mitigation says to \"publish the convention early and file tracking issues in COO projects\". The tension is noted; if external adoption delay materializes, the implementing team may pull the tracking-issue filing forward without changing epic structure.\n90\t- **CI lint rule does not exist yet**: the strategy's acceptance criteria are \"measured by CI lint rule or static analysis\" — the lint rule is included as scope of E002 (it does not currently exist). Its repo-wide enforcement must be coordinated with the parallel cloudmanager conversion (E003) to avoid transient CI failures.\n91\t- **Audit percentage is an assumption**: \"75% of log calls embed identity\" needs validation via codebase audit — captured as the first step/AC of E002, not a structural unknown (conversion scope is all call sites regardless).\n92\t- **Log consumer regression validation requires cluster access**: before-merge validation needs a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack (Loki, CloudWatch) and SRE/Support coordination — a human process gate on E002, reflected in its AI signals.\n93\t- **COO adoption is best-effort**: no epic exists for Loki/Tempo Operator adoption; per overlay 0008 this is voluntary external work. Downstream value of the publication epic is not guaranteed by this strategy.\n94\t\n95\t## Tiered Delivery\n96\t\n97\tThe only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption of the published convention. RHOAI-side scope delivers full user value without it (all rhods-operator logs are standardized regardless), and per platform policy RHOAI cannot mandate external adoption — so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is required. All four epics are independent of the external party; COO adoption is tracked via issues filed as part of the docs epic, not gated work.\n98\t\n99\t## Priority Derivation\n100\t\n101\t- E002, E003: P0 — directly implement P0 HLRs. E002 additionally carries bundled P1 HLR7 (same implementation surface; not deferrable as an independent unit).\n102\t- E001: P1 — priority inheritance from the HLR it transitively enables (HLR8, P1). It blocks no P0 work.\n103\t- E004: P1 — docs-authoring priority exception: strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Does not push priority upstream (Rule 11 exception).\n104\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb2518830a4fe5b1","agent_id":"a2f15ca84a888e9e0","session_id":"affdec6a-eca1-48e8-b648-ac73c57fc806","uuid":"81cde8d7-8d9d-4d02-bd9a-992b895e2e92","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:39:34.758Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-84478b8bb95224a2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
