{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bccf84911f8ff31d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs in rhods-operator\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs in rhods-operator\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tApply the same structured log standardization to the rhods-operator's cloudmanager binary, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, which already provides `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. The remaining work is converting message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind`. This is a separate unit of work from the manager-binary conversion so it can be scheduled and released independently — whether it ships in the same release as the manager work or a subsequent one is an open product decision (PM / ODH Operator team), and this epic's scope is identical either way.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) and any shared reconciler error paths specific to the cloudmanager binary.\n38\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`).\n39\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs (kind is not implied by the shared reconciler pattern, so this is a functional requirement here, not just queryability).\n40\t- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness by parsing test logger output for each of the three controllers.\n41\t- Backwards-compatibility validation for cloudmanager logs: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error, confirming the production log consumer on the cloud deployment targets (CloudWatch, where cloudmanager runs on AWS EKS / Azure AKS / CoreWeave) ingests both the old and new formats without configuration changes. This extends the dev-cluster consumer regression validation performed for the manager-binary conversion with a cloudmanager-specific sample.\n42\t- Coordinate with the repo-wide CI lint rule delivered by the manager-binary conversion so enforcement covers cloudmanager paths once its call sites are converted.\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n47\t- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the log call uses structured key-value pairs instead, measured by the repo CI lint rule or static analysis flagging any remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text.\n48\t- Given the shared reconciler already emits `name`/`namespace`, when the conversion is complete, then no regression occurs in those existing fields, measured by unit test confirming both pre-existing and newly added fields are present with correct values.\n49\t- Given the cloudmanager log format changes, when a before/after comparison is run for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error, then structured fields appear and the log consumer on the cloud deployment targets (CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes, measured by before/after log output comparison covering a cloudmanager reconciliation error.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- P0: All rhods-operator (manager binary) reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs — cloudmanager-binary portion.\n54\t- P0: All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8977423f4900153e","agent_id":"a60889b1e32e28126","session_id":"affdec6a-eca1-48e8-b648-ac73c57fc806","uuid":"283e35e2-f197-4e9f-9278-e74d393fcbb9","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:41:55.692Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bccf84911f8ff31d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
